---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { getIdeologies, getThinkers } from '../lib/content';
import { url } from '../lib/url';

const thinkers = await getThinkers();
const ideologies = await getIdeologies();
const count = (status: string) => thinkers.filter((t) => t.data.status === status).length;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="What the Hall of Abstraction is, how thinkers are chosen and categorised, and how profiles are written."
>
  <div class="container">
    <header class="page-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
      <h1>About the Hall</h1>
    </header>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        The Hall of Abstraction is a reading room for ideas. It gathers short, readable profiles of
        the thinkers whose work changed how people reason about truth, ethics, society and
        power, and arranges them by the schools of thought they founded or advanced.
      </p>

      <h2>Who is included</h2>
      <p>
        A thinker earns a place by the depth and reach of their influence: ideas that founded or
        transformed a tradition, that are still argued over, and that shaped how later generations
        thought and lived. The Hall currently holds {thinkers.length} thinkers across
        {' '}{ideologies.length} schools of thought, and it is meant to grow. Many important
        figures are not yet here, and their absence is a matter of time, not a judgement.
      </p>

      <h2>How thinkers are categorised</h2>
      <p>
        Few great minds fit neatly in one box. Each thinker is therefore listed under one
        <strong>primary</strong> ideology, the tradition they are most identified with, and may be
        <strong>tagged</strong> with others. John Locke, for example, is listed under Empiricism but
        also appears on the Liberalism page. Each profile also records who influenced whom, so you
        can follow ideas from one mind to the next.
      </p>
      <p>
        Labels like “Rationalism” or “Existentialism” were often applied after the fact, and some
        thinkers rejected them. Treat them as a map, not a verdict.
      </p>

      <h2>How profiles are written</h2>
      <p>
        Profiles are written for curious general readers, not specialists. Each one cites the
        reference works it draws on, and quotations name their source and translation.
      </p>
      <p>Every profile goes through three stages, and its sidebar says which one it has reached:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>Draft.</strong> The lesson is written with AI assistance from the cited
          sources.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Fact-checked.</strong> Dates, events, quotations, translations and sources are
          checked against reference works, again with AI assistance, and errors are corrected.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Reviewed.</strong> The site's editor has read the whole page and approved it.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        So far, of {thinkers.length} profiles, {count('reviewed')} have been reviewed by the
        editor, {count('fact-checked')} are fact-checked and awaiting review, and
        {' '}{count('draft')} are drafts.
      </p>
      <p>
        Spotted an error or want to suggest a thinker? The project lives on
        <a href="https://github.com/sharmaxhimanshu1-arch/hall-of-abstraction">GitHub</a>, and
        corrections are welcome.
      </p>

      <p><a href={url('/ideologies/')}>Start exploring the ideologies →</a></p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .page-head {
    padding: 3rem 0 1rem;
  }
</style>
